    "title": "Wild anabelomorphy: recovering ramification from the pro-p unit filtration",
    "description": "For a non-archimedean local field K with residue characteristic p, the multiplicative group decomposes as K^\u00d7 \u2245 \u2124 \u00d7 \u03bc_{q-1} \u00d7 (1 + m_K), where q = p^f is the residue-field size and the pro-p group 1 + m_K encodes wild ramification. The tame part \u03bc_{q-1} is rigid: an abstract isomorphism of these cyclic groups already forces equality of (p, f). The proposed program is to determine exactly which arithmetic invariants (the absolute ramification index e, the different, the higher ramification breaks) are forced by a topological group isomorphism 1 + m_K \u2245 1 + m_L of the wild parts. Concretely, model 1 + m_K as a finitely generated \u2124_p-module of rank [K:\u211a_p] plus a torsion part of p-power roots of unity, and prove which of (e, f, number of p-power roots of unity) are isomorphism invariants and which can be traded. The key insight is that the residue torus sees f but is blind to e, so the entire obstruction to reconstructing K from group data must live in the pro-p filtration, making the wild part the decisive carrier of ramification information. Why now? The tame (GL(1) residue) layer is now completely pinned down, isolating the pro-p group as the single remaining unknown and giving a precise, testable target rather than a vague reconstruction question.",
